A||||9||85seNUT||The most power...||||1||
Z||000000||85seNUT||02-27-2001||03:03 AM||||Ok two questions.  does anyone know the correct red line for a 3.4 engine?  Does it differ at all from the 2.8?  And another question.  what is the correct RPM's to shift at to get the most power out of each shift.  (as if i was racing).<BR>thank you||||reg||1
Z||000001||85seNUT||02-27-2001||03:04 AM||||oh by the way it is a 4 speed||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula88||02-27-2001||09:09 AM||||That depends on which 3.4.  Is it an F-body 3.4, 3.4 TDC, 3400SFI?  Still the redline should be around 6000rpm for most.  I believe the 3.4TDC redlines at 6500, and my 99 Grand Am's 3400 redlines at 6200.  Don't know about the earlier F-body engines, though.||||reg||6
Z||000003||ThatRickGuy||02-27-2001||09:26 AM||||The 3.4 will probrably redline arround 6k. It's almost identical to the 2.8, except it has a 3.1 crank, and a larger bore. also the side walls are thicker and 'ribbed' for more integrity, which just lets ya get a bigger bore. As far as shift points, that's up to the specific, you can build up a 3.4 to spin 7000 rpms, but in stock form I would guess, and this is a guess, someplace between 4800-5500, HP and torque cross at 5254rpms (based off the calculation) and both are most likely going to be on the downward slope. so you can sift a little before that and you'll be arround peek power, or you can wind it out a little more and see how much power is lost. If you can get it dynoed you can find out exactly.||||reg||1
Z||000004||ThatRickGuy||02-27-2001||09:31 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ThatRickGuy:<BR><B>The 3.4 will probrably redline arround 6k. It's almost identical to the 2.8, except it has a 3.1 crank, and a larger bore. also the side walls are thicker and 'ribbed' for more integrity, which just lets ya get a bigger bore. As far as shift points, that's up to the specific engine, you can build up a 3.4 to spin 7000 rpms, but in stock form I would guess, and this is a guess, someplace between 4800-5500, HP and torque cross at 5254rpms (based off the calculation) and both are most likely going to be on the downward slope. so you can shift a little before that and you'll be arround peek power, or you can wind it out a little more and see how much power is lost. If you can get it dynoed you can find out exactly.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Standard||02-27-2001||11:02 AM||||The stock 3.4 TDC redlines at 7,000 and can be pushed to (i think) 7,600 without any major problems.  8,000 is a set of stiffer valve springs away.<P>Without ignition upgrades, the pushrods usually fall flat after 5,000.  Even after upgrades, it's not really worth pushing it past 5,500..||||reg||1
Z||000006||Formula88||02-27-2001||11:28 AM||||That may be true about the earlier 3.4L pushrod engines, but the roller cam version of the Series III 3400SFI in my Grand Am will happily rev right into the rev-limiter at about 6300rpm.  With a free flow air intake, it pulls strong all the way to redline.  It does tend to run out of breath above 5500rpm, with the stock air box, but that's pretty restrictive.  With a 9" K&N cone on it, it makes power all the way to redline.  BTW, this engine also has one of the flattest torque curves I've ever seen. I'm hoping to see some conversions for Fiero's in the near future.  I think it would be far superior to the 3.4 out of an F-body.  Not as powerful as the 3800SC, but smaller, lighter, and more fuel efficient.  I'd prefer it over a 3800 non-SC any day.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000007||1FST2M6||02-27-2001||11:53 AM||||for a true answer for YOUR car since every car and motor is a little different go to.  <A HREF="http://www.dynojet.com" TARGET=_blank>www.dynojet.com</A>  find a dyno near you, run your car and then judge by the torque curve where to shift. it's usually about $65 to run your car on the dyno. 3 runs/ baseline/ hp-tq and a roll out or all gear run. and the stock ECU chip will default to 6000rpm redline like any pre-88 car.. if you have an ADS/Hyptertech or Jet chip just pull it 6000 anyway.. you'll see if your car is sucking wind. I've tached up my stock 2.8 to 7200 and it made less HP/Tq at 7200 than it did at 2500. so your only loosing ground taching up the motor that high. my best shift point were at 5000rpm keeping the car in the best torque/hp curve all the time. <BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2m6dyno.jpg" TARGET=_blank>www.fiero.nl/uploads/2m6dyno.jpg</A> <P><BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig1fst.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 02-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||MooCow||02-27-2001||01:24 PM||||Well I also have a 95 3.4 engine in my Fiero, but my tranny is a 4:10 gear ratio.  I have yet to go past 5,000 RPM's just cause its a new engine to me and not broken in yet. I do know that I can almost make it to 60MPH in second if I pushed it to redline, but I always shift around lower RPM's just cause I am afraid to go any higher. Where do you guys normally shift with everyday driving?  I normally shift around 2.5RPM's but I think I need to cahnge it to 3RPM's cause it sounds like I am luging the engine at 2.5RPM's.   Anyone else?<P>                              Thanks Ken <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/kensig_1.jpg"> <BR>87 GT, 3.4 engine with a 4:10 tranny.  Soon will be Viper Yellow!||||reg||1
Z||000009||Alex4mula||02-27-2001||01:57 PM||||Me? I love to hear my engine rev. I shift between 3k to 4.5k most of the time unless I'm sleepy in the morning.<BR>Regarding shift point, other than 1st you want to be on the RPM with most torque for the start of next gear. That means that for 1st you may need to go up to 6K so that when you shift into 2nd it starts around 3-4K. Similar logic for the rest. Shift speed and tranny ratios will have an impact on which RPM specifically to shift too. Go to a dyno, a drag strip or buy the G-tech to get the best results. Otherwise it will be just seat-of-pants gage.||||reg||1
